ay@4newsquare.com&gt;; B.Williams@4newsquare.com &lt;B.Williams@4newsquare.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Defendants’ Submissions and Draft Order – Costs of CMH2&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Thanks Sarah, I’ll adjust to take account of that point about % of incurred costs (but will wait for any other comments before doing so).&nbsp;On the Post-July point:We are 100% on the same page for the scenario if Cs win the PDD trial.On scenario B (Cs lose PDD), I think the difference come from these two parts of your list below:Quantum trial will not happen&nbsp;– when we were discussing this earlier in the year pre-PTR, I recall there was hesitation in the group about whether it would be as simple as this (or at least whether it was something we were happy to express this in these absolute terms). That’s where my concerns about the case for ‘full’ cover originate from. If we are now able (and happy) to say that an adverse outcome in the PDD trial would mean in practice that the T3 trial was immediately vacated that’s fine, but my understanding from previous chats was that it might not be as simple as this, and/or there could be outcomes which were effectively losses but which were not strictly fatal to the T3 trial going ahead? If we can’t assert that T3 would go away immediately upon an adverse PDD judgment, then we can’t say the costs will either, and so that’s where the difference of approach arises from I think.Quantum PTR, Trial Prep and Trial Phases will ever be incurred&nbsp;– if the T3 trial does get vacated at the end of July 2026, then you are correct that the Trial phase shouldn’t be incurred (unless some small part is used for a form of ‘consequentials’ but:The PTR is due to be held in July 2026 I think ([47] of Quantum directions) so there’s a very real chance those costs are incurred, even if the hearing itself gets used for something else at the last minute; andGiven the trial starts in October with summer in between, I anticipate at least some of the trial prep costs will be incurred before the end of July. I expect some tranches of brief will be incurred, and there will also be at least some solicitor work (eg I can see there are trial bundle deadlines in July at [52]-[55]).So, I think the key question is whether or not we can say with confidence that if Cs lose (/ receive an adverse costs order in) T2, that is likely to mean that the T3 trial gets abandoned at the end of July 2026. I’m in PG/LDs hands on that. If that’s something we can say, then yes, I can see the argument that there is unlikely to be a set of circumstances which lead to needing cover for the ‘full’ T3 period. If it is more complicated that that, T3 might still go ahead, that’s where we get the potential for a greater today ATE requirement.But even if it is correct and the T3 trial would get immediately vacated, I don’t think it would necessarily mean that none of Quantum PTR or Trial Prep are incurred. I expect at least some of each would be incurred (and TP is obviously a sizeable sum).&nbsp;ThanksSimon&nbsp;&nbsp;Simon&nbsp;Teasdale​​​​BarristerDirect&nbsp;+442078222119&nbsp;Mobile&nbsp;+447969074414&nbsp;Chambers&nbsp;+442078222000&nbsp;4&nbsp;New&nbsp;Square&nbsp;Chambers​Lincoln's&nbsp;Inn,&nbsp;London&nbsp;WC2A&nbsp;3RJFollow&nbsp;us&nbsp;on&nbsp;YouTube | LinkedIn​Discover&nbsp;our Events&nbsp;Privilege and Confidentiality Notice:This email is private as between the sender and the intended recipient. The information contained in this message and any file that may be attached to it are confidential between those parties and may be privileged. Any other party who receives it in error is not permitted to read, use, copy, discuss or pass on the information contained in either the message or its attachments, nor are they entitled to take any action in reliance on the contents. Therefore, if you are not the intended recipient you are kindly requested to contact me immediately by telephone on the numbers given above.Members of 4 New Square Chambers are regulated by the Bar Standards Board.NOTE TO SOLICITORS: ALL PAPERS RECEIVED FOR MEMBERS OF CHAMBERS WILL BE SENT FOR SECURE SHREDDING&nbsp;UPON COMPLETION OF WORK&nbsp;UNLESS YOU SPECIFICALLY REQUEST THEIR RETURN.From:&nbsp;Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;06 October 2025 10:27To:&nbsp;Simon Teasdale &lt;S.Teasdale@4newsquare.com&gt;; Sophia Thomson &lt;sthomson@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Fiona Huddleston &lt;FHuddleston@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;spopplewell@leighday.co.uk; Jamie Macfarlane &lt;jmacfarlane@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; KLatham@kingschambers.com; Theo Barclay &lt;T.Barclay@4newsquare.com&gt;; Benjamin Williams KC &lt;B.Williams@4newsquare.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: Defendants’ Submissions and Draft Order – Costs of CMH2&nbsp;EXTERNAL EMAILHi Simon,Thanks so much for updating the note. It's looking really good. Just two things.At [40][footnote 13][49a.], PG previously applied a 60%&nbsp;factor to incurred costs (Rowe &amp; Ors v Ingenious Media Holdings plc &amp; Ors&nbsp;[2020] EWHC 235 (Ch) and B v CD&nbsp;[2021] EWHC 741 (Comm)). This time, we have applied 50%, given the criticisms the Defendants themselves made against each other on incurred costs (notably Mr Andrew Lyons against Mercedes).I’m still struggling to follow the logic of why the Claimants would need to secure LOI for the Defendants’ work incurred post–July 2026&nbsp;(i.e. after the PDD judgment).I’m not suggesting a further SFC application in July, my thinking is that one would never be required.&nbsp;My understanding is as follows:A. Claimants Win PDD (wholly or partially)No (or limited) adverse costs order for PDD in Defendants'&nbsp;favour → some or all existing ATE cover remains unused&nbsp;for that purpose.Quantum trial proceeds in October, but the PDD-era ATE resource can be “recycled”&nbsp;to cover the Defendants future adverse costs exposure (e.g. Quantum PTR, Trial Prep, Trial).Net result:&nbsp;No need for additional&nbsp;ATE or LOI cover for post July 2026 if the existing cover (from PDD) exceeds or matches quantum-phase risk (which does).B. Claimants Lose PDDAdverse costs order for PDD&nbsp;→ Current ATE is needed to satisfy that liability;&nbsp;therefore Claimants need sufficient ATE to cover costs up to July 2026.Quantum trial will not happen, since it follows logically from PDD success.Therefore: Quantum PTR, Trial Prep, and Trial phases will never be incurred -&nbsp;and there is no risk of further adverse costs&nbsp;after July 2026.Net result:&nbsp;No additional ATE or LOI needed beyond July 2026 (and any already-obtained for post-July 2026&nbsp;may be wasted, i.e. irrecoverable premium expense).Either way, the conclusion seems to be that no further ATE or LOI cover is required for the Defendants’ adverse costs post–July 2026 on any outcome.Happy to jump on a call and be corrected if I’m missing the point somewhere.Kind regards,Sarah​​​​Sarah&nbsp;BinghamLegal&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7484&nbsp;085&nbsp;187This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our website. For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our Privacy Policy.From:&nbsp;Simon Teasdale &lt;S.Teasdale@4newsquare.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Monday, October 06, 2025 09:40To:&nbsp;Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sophia Thomson &lt;sthomson@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Fiona Huddleston &lt;FHuddleston@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;spopplewell@leighday.co.uk&nbsp;&lt;spopplewell@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Jamie Macfarlane &lt;jmacfarlane@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; KLatham@kingschambers.com&nbsp;&lt;KLatham@kingschambers.com&gt;; T.Barclay@4newsquare.com&nbsp;&lt;T.Barclay@4newsquare.com&gt;; B.Williams@4newsquare.com&nbsp;&lt;B.Williams@4newsquare.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Defendants’ Submissions and Draft Order – Costs of CMH2&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Dear Sarah and Sophia,&nbsp;Thanks again for your comments on the Note.&nbsp;Further to the below, I have now re-written the section at the end on ATE. It ultimately flags the same risks of not obtaining ‘full’ cover, and identifies that as the safest approach if the aim is to avoid an application, but also recognises the reasons why many firms may not want to go down that route, and notes the approach which PG/LD have taken (as I understand it – but please check what I say at the end of [47] in particular to make sure I have got that correct).&nbsp;Hopefully, that solves the difficulty and gives all cohorts the ability to make their own decisions based on their own circumstances (which will vary now more than ever, given that in previous phases most cohorts will have needed ‘full’ cover for the Cs own purposes in any event). And I expect, given the deadline for confirming positions is this Friday, most firms will have their plan in place already anyway. I’m keen not to have the ATE/security aspect take over the note which is really just meant to be about how the budgets work, hence why I have slightly tried to pull it back to that at the end.&nbsp;Anyway – if PD/LG are happy with the attached, it is ready for distribution. If there are further aspects which anyone would like to discuss, feel free to send them via email or call, I will be around this afternoon to assist if needed.&nbsp;ThanksSimon&nbsp;Simon&nbsp;Teasdale​​​​BarristerDirect&nbsp;+442078222119&nbsp;Mobile&nbsp;+447969074414&nbsp;Chambers&nbsp;+442078222000&nbsp;4&nbsp;New&nbsp;Square&nbsp;Chambers​Lincoln's&nbsp;Inn,&nbsp;London&nbsp;WC2A&nbsp;3RJFollow&nbsp;us&nbsp;on&nbsp;YouTube | LinkedIn​Discover&nbsp;our Events&nbsp;Privilege and Confidentiality Notice:This email is private as between the sender and the intended recipient. The information contained in this message and any file that may be attached to it are confidential between those parties and may be privileged. Any other party who receives it in error is not permitted to read, use, copy, discuss or pass on the information contained in either the message or its attachments, nor are they entitled to take any action in reliance on the contents. Therefore, if you are not the intended recipient you are kindly requested to contact me immediately by telephone on the numbers given above.Members of 4 New Square Chambers are regulated by the Bar Standards Board.NOTE TO SOLICITORS: ALL PAPERS RECEIVED FOR MEMBERS OF CHAMBERS WILL BE SENT FOR SECURE SHREDDING&nbsp;UPON COMPLETION OF WORK&nbsp;UNLESS YOU SPECIFICALLY REQUEST THEIR RETURN.From:&nbsp;Simon TeasdaleSent:&nbsp;03 October 2025 17:36To:&nbsp;'Sarah Bingham' &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sophia Thomson &lt;sthomson@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Fiona Huddleston &lt;FHuddleston@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;spopplewell@leighday.co.uk; Jamie Macfarlane &lt;jmacfarlane@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; KLatham@kingschambers.com; Theo Barclay &lt;T.Barclay@4newsquare.com&gt;; Benjamin Williams KC &lt;B.Williams@4newsquare.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Defendants’ Submissions and Draft Order – Costs of CMH2&nbsp;Thanks allI’ve accepted in most of those amends, and made a few more (though largely cosmetic). The reference to the section in the CMH1 judgment about the aggregate total of Ds’ costs was deliberate, just because I think it is important to remind the wider cohort of firms why the totals look so different between Ds and Cs.&nbsp;A few responses to some of the comments below in case helpful:Sophia:In terms of the SFC directions, if you think the sealed version will be out on Monday, fine to wait rather than circulating the draft. Otherwise, I think it makes sense to circulate the final draft just to make extra sure everyone has a copy (given the deadline next Friday).On Pan-NOx recoveries, I don’t think we can safely go further than [21] at this stage (which is essentially in the same terms as last year’s note). It is necessarily uncertain, as is going to have to be approach commercially/on a case-by-case basis in the event of settlements. It seems to be that we can’t say more about allocation at this stage because the court is using it as a category precisely because it wants to avoid any conclusions about allocation at this stage.I’m minded not to raise the ‘Olie Lawson’ point because it is really a point which only applies to Milberg, and I am sure they will have read the judgment and taken note of that point. I think adding it in to a Note for everyone is just going to look like singling them out.Sarah:I’ve just deleted that [28]-[30] section you were worried about. It was an important point to make last year but, on reflection, I expect it has been overtaken somewhat by the conversations which have been had since then and the further arrangements put in place for ‘sharing’ the pot (or not, as the case may be), so better just to leave it out now.Re your comment on the way to deal with Pan-NOx divisions etc, I agree those are probably the main options, but I think the problem with it is that the examples work on the assumption of all Cs being entitled to recover their costs. In a scenario where some Cs are entitled to recover their costs but others are not, the ‘access’ to the budgeted sums would necessarily shift (at least formally, for the purposes of which Ds which costs were sought from). It might be that the same principles could be applied, even if to a different set of claimant groups, or that sums are shared internally in a way which is different to the way that they are strictly recovered from the other side. In any event, I certainly agree with you that it’s not something we are really able to get into in this Note – it is a much more difficult question for another day.On Non-Lead time, I have added a line in [33] to highlight the fact that the court refused to countenance any more than “extremely limited” costs from Non-Leads in these budgets, and that this echoed the approach exhibited at CMH1 (even if not necessarily apparent in the CMH1 judgment). I’m not sure I can go further than that as clearly they have accounted for some Non-Lead costs in there, just extremely limited sums (though, again, I have a degree of sympathy with the NLs there because I still don’t think the court has fully appreciated the full extent of what counts as ‘common costs’, and wrongly assumes that ‘individual costs’ are the solution).On SteerCo time, the position is trickier because in principle there is nothing to stop a lot of work being delegated to SteerCo firms and it being recovered for within the budgets – but I have slightly beefed up the relevant paragraph to make clear that delegated work is really what the court will have in mind for substantial SteerCo recovery, not just duplication/additional people reviewing etc.&nbsp;However, I think I need to give the points about ATE a bit more thought over the weekend, with a view to finalising those on Monday. I’m cautious about suggesting it is risk-free to only get cover up to July 2026 figures in the approved budget. It strikes me that – at the very least- it makes it much more likely we will have a real fight with Ds about security this time, and the point about revisiting ATE post-PDD Trial outcome seemed to get very little traction with Cockerill when we raised it at the PTR in an attempt to get the hearing of any applications pushed back. The potential prejudice to Ds is that, if we don’t get the additional cover they say we may need, it would be late September/start of October before they’d be able to get a SFC hearing listed, at which point we are into the T3 Trial. So it’s not so much about how much of T3/G2’s costs have been incurred by the time the PDD Judgment is out, it is how much has been incurred once we’ve got to a hearing that has only been listed once the judgment has come out. I got the impression at the PTR when considering the timetable that that was what Cockerill was concerned about, and she was keen to deal with T3/G2 security once and for all in January.However, it may be that I can set out what approach has been taken by the Lead Solicitors, and that there is value in alignment on these points, and a reasonable argument that it would be unjust for the court to hold that an order for further security would be unjust, but I think I’ll have to flag that there is a real chance the court sides with Ds on the quantum point (and the concerns about how quickly any further SFC hearing could be listed pre-T3 trial if needed). The extent to which cohorts are actually exposed to applications will vary and so some may want to play it safe/safer (particularly if the sums involved for them are smaller).But I’ve taken those comments onboard and do understand the issue which the timing causes here. I’ll give that some further thought over the weekend and update on Monday.If helpful to discuss any of this, just let me know.&nbsp;Thanks,Simon&nbsp;&nbsp;From:&nbsp;Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;03 October 2025 16:22To:&nbsp;Simon Teasdale &lt;S.Teasdale@4newsquare.com&gt;; Sophia Thomson &lt;sthomson@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Fiona Huddleston &lt;FHuddleston@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: spopplewell@leighday.co.uk; Jamie Macfarlane &lt;jmacfarlane@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; KLatham@kingschambers.com; Theo Barclay &lt;T.Barclay@4newsquare.com&gt;; Benjamin Williams KC &lt;B.Williams@4newsquare.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: Defendants’ Submissions and Draft Order – Costs of CMH2&nbsp;EXTERNAL EMAILHi Simon,&nbsp;LD and PG have reviewed and made some comments (and a few minor track changes).&nbsp;&nbsp;Over to you - many thanks,Sarah&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Sarah&nbsp;BinghamLegal&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7484&nbsp;085&nbsp;187This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our website. For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our Privacy Policy.From:&nbsp;Simon Teasdale &lt;S.Teasdale@4newsquare.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Friday, October 03, 2025 12:31To:&nbsp;Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sophia Thomson &lt;sthomson@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Fiona Huddleston &lt;FHuddleston@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;spopplewell@leighday.co.uk&nbsp;&lt;spopplewell@leighday.co.uk&gt;; Jamie Macfarlane &lt;jmacfarlane@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; KLatham@kingschambers.com&nbsp;&lt;KLatham@kingschambers.com&gt;; T.Barclay@4newsquare.com&nbsp;&lt;T.Barclay@4newsquare.com&gt;; B.Williams@4newsquare.com&nbsp;&lt;B.Williams@4newsquare.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: Defendants’ Submissions and Draft Order – Costs of CMH2&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Thanks both – and don’t worry, I know there’s a lot going on at the moment, and this&nbsp; is (rightly) quite low in the order of priorities.&nbsp;Given Sarah’s availability, I’ll plan to finalise it on Monday morning.&nbsp;ThanksSimon&nbsp;Simon&nbsp;Teasdale​​​​BarristerDirect&nbsp;+442078222119&nbsp;Mobile&nbsp;+447969074414&nbsp;Chambers&nbsp;+442078222000&nbsp;4&nbsp;New&nbsp;Square&nbsp;Chambers​Lincoln's&nbsp;Inn,&nbsp;London&nbsp;WC2A&nbsp;3RJFollow&nbsp;us&nbsp;on&nbsp;YouTube | LinkedIn​Discover&nbsp;our Events&nbsp;Privilege and Confidentiality Notice:This email is private as between the sender and the intended recipient. The information contained in this message and any file that may be attached to it are confidential between those part