Matt - thanks for your email separately. I'm taking the liberty to relay Giles W KC advice re consultant point:
&nbsp;
There is no inherent regulatory bar on a solicitor at another firm being engaged to work as a consultant on a particular matter.&nbsp; From a conduct of litigation perspective, I don’t see any problem with this.
&nbsp;
There are of course a raft of issues that any such consultancy arrangement would need to address, e.g. how to deal with conflicts of interest, lines of supervision/responsibility, client consent, the terms of
 the consultancy engagement, and no doubt various other issues.&nbsp; There will always be questions that can be asked about these arrangements, but (a) I’m not sure that there would be any real justification for Slaughters probing into them, and (b) there is no
 reason why these points can’t be satisfactorily dealt with by a suitable consultancy agreement, such that it can work without any giving rise to any regulatory problems.
