Subject: Quantum resource 

&nbsp;




Hi Anna,


&nbsp;


Now&nbsp;that we are back in the Lead Solicitor seat (yay!), I (with the support of the other case leads) want&nbsp;to propose the idea of bringing Pete back on
 quantum, which in my&nbsp;(and the case leads')&nbsp;opinion is firmly&nbsp;in the best interests of all the Claimants. We did suggest this as part of the AA but appreciate this was not a priority for LD/PG at the time. Per yesterday's ruling, we continue to be the Lead
 Solicitors so we don't see any reason why us delegating work to him to assist us on this workstream would cause any issue.&nbsp;


With the quantum workload set to ramp up over the next few months (IPOQs are due on 21 November&nbsp;along with a host of wider strategy decisions and other
 court deadlines), having Pete’s guidance and support would be incredibly valuable, especially as you, Martyn, and Michael (who has been my sounding board on quantum) will now be even busier with the PDD trial&nbsp;and the other associated non NOx trial workstreams
 (per Boddy et al's comments about applications for the 21 November hearing). Pete not only has greater availability, but also the advantage of having previously been highly engaged&nbsp;on quantum issues at PG, both from a settlement and trial perspective.&nbsp;He is
 uniquely positioned to step into the role with his accumulated wealth of knowledge on PDD issues (which only the current PDD case leads can match) and he would be imperative to us moving quantum forward on the right foot. This is true in respect of both strategic
 calls on procedural matters and legal issues. With the other case leads tied up on PDD and decisions needing to be made now, it is useful to bring him in asap.&nbsp;It is a very complex workstream, which he worked on for years. He’d be a natural fit to hit the
 ground running and a perfect addition to the team.&nbsp;


This is more so relevant&nbsp;and pressing&nbsp;given that Fiona (the LD partner on quantum) is due to take her sabbatical at the end of the year, and her replacement
 has only recently been moved to the NOx litigation. We are also facing a gap on the counsel side with both Adam and Chloe no longer involved, after having spent the past year on quantum. Pete also has the ear and trust of the counsel team which is another
 key reason why he should be brought back in.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


Bringing Pete back would be a great way to retain some continuity and momentum, and I think it would really help steady the ship through this next sustained
 period. Equally, not taking the opportunity to re-engage his experience and expertise, with approaching critical deadlines, would not be in the Claimants' best interests&nbsp;and it will only serve to create greater issues with people reading in.&nbsp;You only have
 to ask some of the staff who have come over from BHP as to how long it has taken them to get up to speed on NOx in general and capacity to get up to speed is a commodity that we cannot afford right now.&nbsp;


&nbsp;


Please let me know your thoughts before I raise it with Martyn. I understand, however, that he would be supportive of this. We already know that Pete
 has long established rapport with the Judges, and the Defendants, and his presence on the Quantum workstream, and specifically at the Progress Meetings, will be something of immense and unique value. Given the approaching IPOQs we need to bring Pete back very
 quickly.&nbsp;&nbsp;


&nbsp;


Pri

































