Hi Raminder,
&nbsp;
Many thanks again for your email. I have now had an opportunity to review the draft response (and have spoken to Jonathan Lawrence who has in turn spoken to Fergal) and from an employment perspective I had
 the following initial comments (I have reproduced an extract from PG’s whistleblowing policy below which informs some of these thoughts):-
&nbsp;

Process: We are rushing back to them with an answer to all their points very fast and, as a result, there
 could be an allegation that PG has not investigated the matter properly and, as such, that they argue that PG have committed a fundamental breach of contract entitling them to walk out of their employment immediately. I appreciate that this is a weak argument,
 however we do not want to give them any opportunity here – particularly given that our threat would be to injunct them to hold them to their notice periods and PG were reluctant, from a commercial position, to do that with Gallagher. Query therefore whether
 it would be better to send a holding response now with a promise to investigate and respond fully as soon as possible - say by the end of the week? This would demonstrate that a proper process is being followed but that we were prioritising it such that they
 could not argue any delay. The only caveat to this, is a comment from Fergal and Jonathan which is that
the implication that the Board might need to investigate whether Alicia (a member of the Board) had mislead the Court rather than knowing already that she had not. The way to mitigate that could be by saying
 that the Board consider the allegations are baseless on the basis of their current knowledge, but in order to follow their proper process, will investigate them properly in accordance with the policy.Strategy: Do we want to rush back in any event? Subject to your instructions, I would have thought that
 we want to string this out as long as possible (subject to not delaying too long as per point 1 above) so that they don’t jump ship. As I understand it, the longer we get into the NOx litigation the more difficult it will be for them to have the time for them
 to leave/ join JLG/ and get involved in the CRO.Approach: Although I entirely agree that Burke and co are most likely to be raising allegations maliciously
 and/or not in good faith contrary to the policy, (i) making disclosures in good faith is no longer a requirement to raise a protected disclosure; and (ii) given the risk identified in point 1 above I wonder whether, strategically, we should be so pugnacious
 in this response? Given that we don’t want to give them even half a chance to walk out I wonder whether adopting a more neutral approach is better at this stage? E.g. “thank you once again for raising these points. As you know we take any concerns raised
 very seriously and encourage employees to raise matters with which they are not happy. To that end we have thoroughly investigated your concerns and reached the following findings….” And at the end: “we trust that this answers your concerns.” For
 this response I would steer away from cross referencing Burke’s own email asking to leave early, beyond, perhaps, a general reference to the policy and that malicious disclosures are not appropriate that we could weave in. I appreciate that there is a balance
 to be struck here between shutting them down and not walking into a trap whereby they just up sticks.Form of response: Your whistleblowing policy does not actually require that a formal response be provided
 – just that an investigation is done. The extent of the obligation is: “and formal meeting(s)
may be arranged to clarify what action or remediation measures will be taken”. This is entirely standard for a whistleblowing policy. Therefore, technically, no response need be provided. I appreciate however that this
is a case where we do want a comprehensive response.Person conducting the investigation: There is no strict obligation to have an independent person conduct
 the investigation but clearly it is fairer if this is done. I appreciate that there may be little appetite however to have someone independent come in and look at everything&nbsp; - unless this would work better from a tactical perspective as it would build appropriate
 and understandable delay.
