d. Is there anything further we can provide to them – are there previous written advices from Counsel that seem to be alluded to in Counsel’s email that can be shared? This is what we have and have been shared with Toremis (+ last weeks’ note). Is the plan for PG to draft a more detailed note that Counsel may review as they suggest?&nbsp;We have spoken to ten insurers about these top ups, including Toremis and Meridian. Litica, VALE, Ignite, HUL and Arcadian have formally declined the top-ups. In the main they have pointed to their existing line size on PG’s policy and the existing insurance already placed on the NOx emissions cases generally. We have also gone to other insurers (Mosaic, IGI and Thomas Miller), who we are waiting to hear back from regarding their response.&nbsp;As a preliminary point, Mosaic have just come back to understand whether PG are able to insure these matters on behalf of the claimants (the same structure as BHP)? They can only cover 30 consumers per year as policyholders and so having PG/the claimants as joint policyholders will not work for them. Can you confirm this works for you?&nbsp;Kind regards,&nbsp;James Burke&nbsp;Associate Director (Solicitor)Litigation and Contingent Risk FINEX Global&nbsp;WTWOn behalf of Willis Limited, 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ (registered number 181116)On behalf of Willis Towers Watson SA/NV, Quai des Vennes, 4020, Liège, Belgium (registered as a branch in the UK at 51 Lime Street, London, EC3M 7DQ UK Branch Number BR021056) in relation to all EEA-regulated business&nbsp;D: +44 (0)20 31248037M: +44 (0)7831 162591&nbsp;james.burke2@wtwco.comwww.wtwco.com&nbsp;Financial, Professional, and Executive Risks (FINEX)Your Trusted Partner in Managing Complex Risk: Expertly navigating Litigation Risk, Cyber, Directors &amp; Officers, Financial Institutions, Mergers &"}

