See below for Clyde’s advice on the Bingham email. The attached email further considers @Neil Pigott’s queries sent earlier.&nbsp;In summary, Charlie confirms yet again that we do not need their consent, but the offer of a meeting to explain our position is clearly the most pragmatic way forward. &nbsp;Thank you R&nbsp;&nbsp;&#8203;&#8203;&#8203;&#8203;Raminder&nbsp;GrewalGlobal&nbsp;HR&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;718From: Urquhart, Charles &lt;charles.urquhart@clydeco.com&gt;Date: Monday, 6 October 2025 at 10:23To: Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc: Riceman, Sarah &lt;Sarah.Riceman@clydeco.com&gt;Subject: RE: LD Agency [CC-UNITEDKINGDOM.131211.10761935.FID8873487]CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Good morning Raminder,&nbsp;I have now had a chance to look at this. My thoughts are below:-&nbsp;General Advice&nbsp;My advice is as follows:-&nbsp;Clearly in a perfect world obtaining an employee’s consent/ buy in to any sort of change is always preferable – however in my view this is not necessary here and I entirely understand and appreciate why we would not want to have to do this in any event;The reason why consent is not necessary is:-Clause 4.2(c) of their contracts of employment requires each of them during their employment to “comply with all reasonable and lawful directions given to the Employee by the Company” – in my view working with Martyn Day on ongoing litigation which they have been working hand in glove with him on anyway for several years for a trial that starts next week is a reasonable and lawful direction;Clause 4.2(b) of their contracts of employment requires each of them during their employment to “diligently exercise such powers and perform such duties as may from time to time be assigned to the Employee by the Company;” – this is the same point.Although my view is that Clauses 4.2 (b) and (c) adequately cover the point, there is an additional helpful provision at Clause 4.1 of their contracts which states “The Employee shall serve the Company as a Senior Associate and will report to Legal Director or their nominee.” As such Martyn Day could be designated as the “nominee” for the purposes of their contracts.Please note that there are additional relevant clauses in their contracts which they should be complying with e.g. “to use their best endeavours to promote, protect, develop and extend the business of the Company.” Their actions are, in my view, in clear breach of this.Sarah Bingham’s reference to Clause 4.2(a) in her email is correct albeit she has spun the meaning for her own purposes – the business of the Company remains exactly as it was before and taking some direction from Martyn Day for the common good does not change that;The concept of working on a case with other individuals outside of the firm giving directions to them on a piece of litigation is hardly a novel concept – for example, presumably these individuals do work at the behest of external counsel? No consent is required for those arrangements.&nbsp;Pallas’s Questions&nbsp;Turning specifically to Pallas’s email of 8.39pm last evening and the relevant questions for us:-&nbsp;3. I agree entirely with Pallas’s paragraph 3. If the employees were being seconded they would need to give specific consent but this is not a secondment and falls within the confines of their contracts of employment as explained above;5.2. Given JLG and Gallagher’s behaviour to date, notwithstanding that Nick Johnson confirmed in writing to Alicia that he agreed to the case leads remaining employed by PG until the end of the trial, their ongoing behaviour suggests that they may still be manouevring to escape early. It is therefore essential that all communications with them continue the tone of Anna’s email to them of Saturday 4 October – i.e. respectful/ open to hearing their concerns (however ridiculous they may be) and avoiding any opportunity for them to argue that PG have somehow breached their contracts of employment. That is not to say that we cannot respectfully push back on their points. Pallas’s suggestion of a meeting is eminently sensible albeit that PG should bear in mind that the case leads may try and get a rise out of PG in that meeting on purpose to argue that there has been a break down in relations. On this point, given that the trial starts next week, is there any world now where the case leads could resign and hope to be working at JLG next week for the trial? Something to bear in mind. Something else to consider, if not already done, would be to offer each employee access to PG’s Employee Assistance Programme if it has one – this is because the employees say that they are all stressed – and would therefore demonstrate that you are acknowledging their “stress”.&nbsp;AA Agreement&nbsp;I understand that the word “solely” has been removed from the document. This is helpful in that it deals with one of their points. It also reduces the scale of the delegation of authority to LD which in turn reduces the scope for them to argue that there has been a breach of contract – albeit as explained above I don’t think there has been any such breach;Although I am loathe to suggest any more changes to the Agency agreement given that this needs to be approved by Leigh Day ASAP, in a perfect world a further enhancement could be to designate Martyn Day as the “nominee” in terms of the new reporting line of the Case Leads – so that the language matches up to their contracts of employment. This is not essential however for the purposes of this Agency agreement – it would be enough to tell the employees that Martyn is the person &nbsp;- or one of the people - they need to report to;Otherwise I have no more comments on the Agency agreement.&nbsp;I hope that this helps but if you or Pallas or anyone else have any queries please do not hesitate to ask.&nbsp;Best wishes&nbsp;Charlie&nbsp;PS Just seen your latest email come in. I will review that now&nbsp;Charles&nbsp;UrquhartPartner&nbsp;|&nbsp;Clyde&nbsp;&amp;&nbsp;Co&nbsp;LLPDirect&nbsp;Dial:&nbsp;+44&nbsp;20&nbsp;7876&nbsp;4215&nbsp;|&nbsp;Mobile:&nbsp;+44&nbsp;7810&nbsp;607&nbsp;355The St Botolph Building | 138 Houndsditch | London EC3A 7AR | UKMain +44 207 876 5000 | Fax +44 333 3000 232&nbsp;| www.clydeco.com​My pronouns are: he / him​&nbsp;Follow&nbsp;us&nbsp;on&nbsp;twitter&nbsp;@clydecoemp​​​​&nbsp;From: Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt; Sent: 06 October 2025 07:57To: Urquhart, Charles &lt;charles.urquhart@clydeco.com&gt;; Riceman, Sarah &lt;sarah.riceman@clydeco.com&gt;Subject: Fw: LD Agency&nbsp;Hi. &nbsp;Pls see the &nbsp;Pallas queries on the Employment related points within Bingham’s email. Thank you. R&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for iOS​​​​Raminder&nbsp;GrewalGlobal&nbsp;HR&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;718This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our&nbsp;website.&nbsp;For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our&nbsp;Privacy Policy.From:&nbsp;Matthew Getz &lt;Matthew.Getz@pallasllp.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Sunday, October 5, 2025 8:39 pmTo:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: LD Agency &nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Alicia, this is our recommendation beneath. In short:&nbsp;a.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; We can’t totally ignore the case leadsb.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tell LD (and counsel) the AA can’t be finalised before the PTRc.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Speak to LD after the PTR to make necessary changesd.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Then speak to case leads to tell them about changes, and for the rest they need to get with the picture&nbsp;The emails back and forth with this team is getting us nowhere. We think there needs to be a meeting with them, attended by Alicia and Anna. That obviously can’t be before the PTR, so would need to be on Tuesday at the earliest. But it might even have to be Wednesday. We would prepare you for this.Their overwhelming issue is the delegation of work by LD to individual solicitors at PG. This has always seemed problematic to us, but LD would not budge. We presumed that in practice, things would continue to work as they have done up to now, and Martyn Day seemed to confirm that – but the language remains different.To be clear, we are not conceding the principle that the case leads have the right to consent to this agreement – but they can’t just be ignored.We consider (but should get it cleared by Clydes) that there is nothing inherently wrong or out of the ordinary with an employer telling an employee to do, for a limited period and for specific work, what a responsible third party tells it to do – as long as the employer continues to oversee and supervise (including in the SRA sense) the employee. So if the employer considers the employee is nor being directed properly, it can tell the employee not longer to follow the directions of the third party.But that situation is not precisely what we have, because of the word “solely” in the Agency Agreement, where it relates to LD’s supervision of PG personnel doing the Common Work delegated to them. That makes it appear that only LD will know what the PG employees will do, so PG can no longer supervise them.So what is the way forward? We suggest the following:Tell LD (and counsel) now that we will not be able to agree the AA before the PTR, and that we will need to speak to them after the PTR. During that discussion, give them a summary of the case leads’ concerns, remind them that they want and need to work with the case leads, and the only way we can see this getting anywhere is if we drop the word “solely”. We must also tell them that (subject to Clydes agreeing this is acceptable) PG will let the employees know it is authorising them to do the work at LD’s direction on a limited basis (Common Work only), that PG will retain oversight and supervision, and if PG considers that any direction is improper (eg, a breach of regulations), it will have the right to rescind our instructions to the employees to follow LD’s directions. We see this as the only way forward. Since Natasha has already been in contact with Martyn, it may be that she needs to deliver this message.At the same time, have Clydes look at the case leads’ letter and the agency agreement (with and without the word “solely”) and get their view on the employment law aspects (and how far we can push things). Clydes should also confirm that they see no employment issues with the way we are proposing to resolve this (which should not be an issue). Raminder can draft an employment response, but it should not&nbsp;be sent until we have reviewed it and determined if it should be sent, consistent with our strategy.When you meet the case leads on Tuesday (?), therefore, you will hopefully be able to say PG has amended the agreement, it is clear PG remains their supervisors, and you can be super-robust about everything else.We would also suggest that after that meeting, you have a follow-up with them and Martyn Day, but we have to make sure Martyn is on side before suggesting that.As for the PTR, there is no&nbsp;way we can agree the agency agreement (and any side letter) before then, so we will need to tell the court we’re in the final stages of agreeing it.Ben Williams KC should be told that the agency agreement is being finalised –if necessary, he can say that is being done in consultation with the case leads. He can say that the case leads will be working their notice, which ends 15/19 Dec, but shouldn’t say anything more positive than that.ThanksMatt&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Matthew&nbsp;Getz​​​​PartnerT: +44&nbsp;20&nbsp;4574&nbsp;1006|M: +44&nbsp;79&nbsp;8538&nbsp;1095E:&nbsp; matthew.getz@pallasllp.com110&nbsp;Bishopsgate,&nbsp;London,&nbsp;EC2N&nbsp;4AYpallasllp.com&nbsp;|&nbsp;LinkedInFrom:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;05 October 2025 19:13To:&nbsp;Matthew Getz &lt;Matthew.Getz@pallasllp.com&gt;; Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: LD Agency&nbsp;Hi Matt,&nbsp;@Raminder Grewal&nbsp;has been provided with a copy of the email from Bingham. I don’t think there are any employment issues she’s raised that concern us but she can correct me if I’m wrong.&nbsp;Anna is offline at the moment and I don’t want to bother her but in your consideration can you also please advise as to whether we need to let Ben Williams know that the case leads continue to be upset and he shouldn’t make any positive statements about them tomorrow morning?&nbsp;Many thanks&nbsp;A&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for iOS​​​​Alicia&nbsp;AliniaChief&nbsp;Executive&nbsp;Officer+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;629This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our website. For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our Privacy Policy.From:&nbsp;Matthew Getz &lt;Matthew.Getz@pallasllp.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Sunday, October 5, 2025 7:01:05 PMTo:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;RE: LD Agency&nbsp;CAUTION: This email originated from outside of the organization. Do not click links or open attachments unless you recognize the sender and know the content is safe.&nbsp;Alicia&nbsp;Just so you know, we have been thinking and talking about this, and will get your our responses soon.&nbsp;At some stage, I think you should send the case leads’ email to Raminder, given the employment issues they raise (regardless of what we think of their merits).&nbsp;Matt&nbsp;Matthew&nbsp;GetzPartnerT: +44&nbsp;20&nbsp;4574&nbsp;1006|M: +44&nbsp;79&nbsp;8538&nbsp;1095E:&nbsp; matthew.getz@pallasllp.com110&nbsp;Bishopsgate,&nbsp;London,&nbsp;EC2N&nbsp;4AYpallasllp.com&nbsp;|&nbsp;LinkedIn&nbsp;Pallas Partners LLP is a limited liability partnership registered in England with registered number OC437491, and is authorised and regulated by the Solicitors Regulation Authority of England and Wales (“SRA”) with SRA number 826251. A list of names of the members is available for inspection at our registered address at 110 Bishopsgate, London, EC2N 4AY.&nbsp;The word “partner” denotes a member of&nbsp;Pallas Partners LLP&nbsp;or an employee or consultant with equivalent standing and qualifications.&nbsp;Pallas Partners (US) LLP is a limited liability partnership established in Delaware and registered in New York, located at 75 Rockefeller Plaza, New York, NY, 10019.&nbsp;Attorneys practicing law with Pallas Partners (US) LLP are admitted to practice in New York and their professional conduct is regulated by the New York Rules of Professional Conduct.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;This email (and any attachments) is confidential and may also be legally privileged and is intended solely for the use of the individual or entity addressed above. If you are not the intended recipient, please immediately notify the sender, then delete it from your system. You should not copy it or use it for any purpose, nor disclose its contents to any other person. Please also note that emails sent or received may be monitored to ensure compliance with the law and with our policies.&nbsp;Further regulatory information can be found in the Legal Notices which appear on our website at https://www.pallasllp.com/legal.​​​​From:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;05 October 2025 16:49To:&nbsp;Plate Litigation &lt;plate_litigation@pallasllp.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Raminder Grewal &lt;rgrewal@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Fw: LD Agency&nbsp;Anna needs to be offline for a few hours.&nbsp;&nbsp;We’ve now received another ridiculous email from these people. Can we pls consider how we respond.&nbsp;&nbsp;There are no employment issues that I can identify.&nbsp;&nbsp;I’ve now read it twice and have no idea what they’re asking for.&nbsp;&nbsp;Can the Pallas team please consider an appropriate response?&nbsp;&nbsp;Thank you,&nbsp;A&nbsp;Sent from Outlook for iOS​​​​Alicia&nbsp;AliniaChief&nbsp;Executive&nbsp;Officer+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;629This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you receive this message in error, please contact the sender immediately, destroy the email and any attachments and do not use, copy, store or disclose this email and any attachments for any purpose. Pogust Goodhead does not accept service of documents by electronic means without express prior agreement.&nbsp;Pogust Goodhead is the trading name of&nbsp;PGMBM Law Ltd, a&nbsp;limited liability company registered in England and Wales (no. 6284764) with its&nbsp;registered office at&nbsp;53-64 Chancery House, Chancery Lane, London WC2A 1QS&nbsp;and authorised and regulated by the Solicitors' Regulation Authority. We use the term ‘partner’ to refer to an employee or consultant of Pogust Goodhead with equivalent standing or qualifications. No&nbsp;reference to a partner should be taken to imply partnership for the purposes of the Partnership Act 1890&nbsp;or Limited Liability Partnerships Act 2000. A list of partners is available on our website. For details about what personal information we collect and why, please see our Privacy Policy.From:&nbsp;Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Sunday, October 5, 2025 4:38 pmTo:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Priyanka Gladson &lt;pgladson@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matt Hunt &lt;mhunt@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Georgina Fallows &lt;gfallows@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: LD Agency&nbsp;Dear Anna,&nbsp;Thank you for your email.&nbsp;We have some overarching points to raise before addressing the points in your email regarding the Side Letter Agency Agreement (“SL AA”).&nbsp;First and foremost, we have raised serious and significant issues regarding how traumatic we, and the entire team, are finding this process and that we have genuine concerns for the mental health and well-being of our staff. We further flagged that the proposal creates a two-tier structure amongst the wider team, which risks alienating individual members of the staff. Your email worryingly does not address any of those concerns, nor does it even mention them.&nbsp;We also note that various junior staff within the NOx department have been enlisted to send out correspondence, including on weekends, relating to non-common work and non-PDD Trial matters (see, for example, an email from Hannah Varghese at 15.32 on Saturday, 4 October 2025). Requiring the most junior staff to become actively involved in non-common work issues, while on the other hand attempting to assert that “everything we have done or are intending to do is with the best interests of our clients as well as our staff in mind first and foremost” is simply unreal. Correspondence of that nature and related to non-common work should be sent by an individual not working on critical workstreams.&nbsp;&nbsp;Each of the case leads are committed to finding a co-operative solution to the issues that are being raised. That is why we are proactively engaging in discussions and, in aid of assisting the firm, why we have continued to raise points so as to avoid the firm falling foul of issues that they may otherwise be presented with, e.g. noting potential additional disclosure obligations re. documents referred to in witness statements. You also have said that you “welcome any specific input we wish to contribute”. This is difficult to contemplate as being a genuine offer in circumstances where a) the SL AA was provided by Leigh Day (“LD”), not you nor the Board, despite Pogust Goodhead (“PG”) being our employer; and b) where we have previously sought to provide input (e.g. meeting with Alicia Alinia at 18:00 Monday 29 September 2025) and you have not engaged with it. Instead, you have sent us an email that dismisses our concerns and informs us that our description of the SL AA is incorrect, while at the same time stating that your intention is for this email to now bring these discussions to a conclusion. This is neither helpful nor is it in the spirit of co-operation.&nbsp;&nbsp;For the avoidance of any doubt, we do not consent to the SL AA in its current form. No representations can be made to the Court at the PTR on 6 October that this has been put in place and is agreed. To the extent that you and the Board believe you can bind individuals named in the SL AA (or annexes) by virtue of it being a ‘firm decision’ we disagree for reasons set out below. Every individual subject to the agreement either does not consent to it (the case leads) or, as far as we are aware, has never seen any version of it and so is unable to have provided consent.&nbsp; We accept of course, that Counsel can inform the Court (on 6 October or otherwise) that it is being drafted and the intention is that it will be put in place in due course.&nbsp;&nbsp;If we, as the collective case leads, are to work collaboratively with the Board and you, and HR, to establish how we can move forward amicably, then it requires serious engagement from you and the Board, and not a dismissal of our concerns.&nbsp;&nbsp;We trust that this email will allow for a more collaborative approach going forward.&nbsp;On the points in your email:&nbsp;1.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Purpose and Effect of the Side Letter&nbsp;You explained that PG’s proposed role is intended to operate “exactly as envisaged” within the existing Pan-NOx AA framework, and that the SL AA will simply implement a delegation of common work from LD to PG personnel. If that were indeed the case, a separate SL AA would not be necessary; indeed, this accords with our own understanding that the new arrangement is entirely novel.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;You say this agreement is to “ensure that [we] can continue to work as currently on the existing work plans.” Having reviewed the SL AA, it is clear that it goes further than the delegation provisions in the Pan-NOx AA, and that is precisely why it requires a standalone agreement. The SL AA imposes materially different terms in at least two key respects:&nbsp;&nbsp;a. Formal separation of supervision and control&nbsp;The SL AA introduces an unusual structure whereby PG personnel will perform common work solely under the direction of LD, but remain employed and supervised by PG. Indeed, we understand that the Board intends for PG personnel to carry out generic/firm-specific work under PG’s direction. This formalises a split between line management and professional control that is not addressed, let alone authorised, in the Pan-NOx AA. That agreement permits delegation of work to another firm, not delegation of the supervision of one firm’s employees to another while the employer retains all employment obligations.&nbsp;&nbsp;b. Governance over PG’s internal personnel decisions&nbsp;The SL AA (via Annex B) requires LD’s consultation and agreement before PG can add or remove PG personnel from common work. That means PG cannot unilaterally deploy or reassign its own staff in this litigation without LD’s approval. This imposes external governance over PG’s workforce, a control that is not contemplated in the Pan-NOx AA and which cuts across PG’s autonomy as an employer.&nbsp;&nbsp;The SL AA changes the status quo in material ways such that it cannot be said that what is being proposed is “exactly what we have all been working under” or that “this will not practically change much day to day.”&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;2.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Tension with Employment Contracts&nbsp;Because the SL AA goes beyond what is authorised in the Pan-NOx AA, it creates direct tension with the terms of PG employment contracts. You advise that “there will be no change to your employment contract” and that “consent by employees is not necessary for PG to execute the agency agreement.” We respectfully disagree.&nbsp;Clause 2(b) of the SL AA states that “PG Personnel shall perform such Common Work … solely under the direction of, and reporting solely to, Leigh Day.” This directly conflicts with multiple provisions that we presume appear in each individual employee contract:&nbsp;&nbsp;a. Duty of obedience and supervision&nbsp;Under Clause 4/Duties of the employment contract (which we presume is a standard term of contract for all PG employees), PG employees are contractually required to comply with PG’s directions and report to PG line managers. They are to “devote the whole of their time, attention and abilities to the business of the Company.” The SL AA seeks to override that by imposing a structure in which we report solely to LD in respect of a major portion of our work, without any formal secondment or variation of contract.&nbsp;&nbsp;b. Regulatory oversight&nbsp;The split between day-to-day supervision (by LD) and formal employment responsibility (by PG) creates regulatory ambiguity under the SRA Code of Conduct. Who is the supervising solicitor? Are there two supervising solicitors, one from Leigh Day and another from PG? Which firm bears responsibility for ensuring compliance with professional standards?&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;These are not academic questions - they carry regulatory and indemnity implications.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;c. Consent and legal clarity&nbsp;The arrangement introduces a dual-control model, but without staff consent, proper transparency, or any framework setting out lines of accountability. As PG remains the employer, yet LD directs our work in practice, the legal and ethical basis for this arrangement is unclear, particularly when conflicts, confidentiality obligations, or errors arise.&nbsp;&nbsp;3.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Professional Indemnity Insurance Risk&nbsp;The SL AA structure also raises serious questions around insurance cover. If PG personnel are performing common work solely under LD’s direction (as the SL AA confirms), then it is not clear whose professional indemnity (“PI”) insurance would respond in the event of a professional error. This is in circumstances where PG remains the/our employer and is responsible for holding PI cover for its staff. But LD&nbsp;is envisaged as exercising the day to-day control and making the litigation decisions. The SRA expects supervision and insurance liability to be aligned. That is not the case here.&nbsp;&nbsp;This is not hypothetical. A clear example of this risk occurred this week when LD made a costs offer to the Mercedes Defendants without PG’s knowledge or approval from PG's insurer - despite the fact that PG personnel had prepared the underlying work. If that offer proves problematic, it is unclear whether PG or LD (or both) would be liable and, more importantly, whose insurer would be on risk. That ambiguity creates risk for both firms and undermines the clarity of the delegation framework set out in the Pan-NOx AA.&nbsp;&nbsp;This is precisely why the Pan-NOx AA structured delegation at the firm level: to avoid splitting supervision and risk in a way that confuses accountability. The SL AA unpicks that structure and introduces unnecessary exposure.&nbsp;&nbsp;We refer you to 17(a) of the PTR skeleton argument filed by Mercedes where the Defendants have drawn the Court’s attention to this issue.&nbsp;&nbsp;4.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; Client Consent&nbsp;If PG wishes for us and other PG staff to be instructed by, and report solely to, a firm other than PG, then this requires our express consent, and (we believe) the informed consent of the clients whom we act on behalf of (which will not only be just the clients of PG, but&nbsp; may extent to all clients within every GLO if the work is to be Pan-NOx). If PG personnel are to carry out work on those clients’ cases under the direction of a different firm, the clients must be told who is supervising that work and must provide informed instructions to that effect. We refer you to Rule 1.4 of the Code of Conduct, which requires solicitors to ensure clients are in a position to make informed decisions about their representation. You cannot unilaterally impose this on us, or on any of the PG staff, nor PG’s clients as your email&nbsp; suggests you can.&nbsp;&nbsp;You cannot unilaterally impose a change of supervisory firm on your employees or on the clients, as your email equally suggests. The assumption that this arrangement can simply be implemented without individual or client-level engagement is not supported by the regulatory framework. This concern is not novel: post our comments in this regard, this is also expressly identified in the skeleton argument filed by Mercedes at paragraph 17(b), where they raise the potential for regulatory impropriety in precisely these circumstances.&nbsp;Turning to other points specifically raised in your email:&nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; You note that we are obliged to work our notice periods. Thank you for pointing this out again, although we note it is not consistent with representations that PG has made to the Defendants and Court that the timing of our last days are subject to ongoing discussion. You go on to say that once our notice period has expired, we are free to commence working for JLG, should we so choose. Collectively, none of us have informed you of our plans post-PG.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; You have advised that LD and counsel have approved this solution. Please provide us with this correspondence (including the advice the firm has taken from counsel – as originally requested). We note that as per Martyn Day’s email on 3 October at 13.56 that “Alicia has asked that we leave these issues for PG to sort out with the team. We await the outcome of those discussions and so no need for the meeting this afternoon.” In circumstances where LD are waiting for PG to “sort out these issues with the team” and that they will “await the outcome”, we fail to understand how LD have approved the solution.&nbsp;&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; You advise that you are “now interviewing so with time this work-stream will be handed over”. Please explain which work-stream? Does this relate to non-common work?&nbsp;We repeat that we are keen to try and work together to find a collaborative solution to these issues, not least because of the importance of the upcoming PDD Trial beginning in a little over a week’s time and for which we are all deeply invested in and, in truth, where all of this is causing a huge and detrimental distraction. However, it is plain to us that the SL AA is plainly more than a simple delegation mechanism. It alters the control and supervision structure, restricts PG’s governance over its own staff, creates clear tension with employment contracts and regulatory obligations, and places work done on behalf of the PG&nbsp;clients under the instruction of an external law firm (without any proposal for client consent to be obtained). If there is to be a material change, it needs to be handled transparently and within a proper legal framework. We raise these points constructively and in good faith, with the shared goal of ensuring compliance and continuing to act in our clients’ best interests.&nbsp;We trust these concerns now demonstrate that the issues raised in the draft SL AA document need to be engaged with, and we look forward to hearing from you on those issues and how they can be addressed, such that we can move forward, together.&nbsp;&nbsp; The Case Leads&nbsp;Michael Burke, Priyanka Gladson, Matthew Hunt, Sarah Bingham &amp; Georgina Fallows&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Sarah&nbsp;BinghamLegal&nbsp;Director+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7484&nbsp;085&nbsp;187From:&nbsp;Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Saturday, October 04, 2025 14:12To:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Priyanka Gladson &lt;pgladson@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matt Hunt &lt;mhunt@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Georgina Fallows &lt;gfallows@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;Re: LD Agency&nbsp;All,I&nbsp;have been discussing with Alicia and it may be worth clarifying some matters.&nbsp;First, it is important to clarify precisely how the Agency Agreement will work – which is not as you describe.&nbsp; We hope this gives you immediate comfort with the approach.PG’s proposed role is exactly as envisaged in the Pan NOx Agency Agreement, which we have all been working under: PG will, as authorised by the Pan NOx Agency Agreement, act by authorisation of LD with respect to the Common Work delegated to it by LD.&nbsp; The Agency Agreement will enable PG to continue working on the liability trial and quantum workstream while you remain with us working your notice period - this agreement&nbsp;is intended to ensure that you can continue to work as currently on the existing work plans with the Lead Solicitor. &nbsp;With Alicia, I will continue to have strategic input and meaningful involvement.&nbsp;It is a pragmatic solution to the various concerns.Whilst LD will retain the title of sole Lead Solicitor, this will not practically change much day to day.&nbsp; LD will be required to consult with us in connection with decisions and we expect matters to continue in the manner they have to date -&nbsp;your working relationships with LD will be unchanged in that regard. Structurally and to secure PG’s continued involvement in the cases, PG employees will be delegated work by Leigh Day. You know better than most that PG and LD have been working together for years, including on behalf of many other solicitor firms, and&nbsp;this Agency Agreement will mean that PG to will continue doing so whilst not retaining the title of Lead Solicitor.&nbsp;For the avoidance of doubt, there will be no change to your employment contract and Leigh Day do not and will have no employer-employee relationship with you.&nbsp; In the circumstances consent by employees is not necessary for PG to execute the agency agreement, as this is a structural rather than substantive change it should not be an issue.As continuing PG employees you will continue to work on the Non-Common Work streams as required and directed solely by PG (which includes matters related to the non-Lead Cases, which are not subject to the Agency Agreement).&nbsp;If you have any regulatory concerns that you wish to inform the clients of, please do let us know specifically what they are. In addition we are now interviewing so with time this work-stream will be gradually handed over - in a sensible way that serves our clients' best interests.&nbsp;We, LD and counsel have approved this solution which adheres with and is entirely consistent with the existing legal framework, offering the best path forward in the best interests of our clients. We also welcome any specific input you may wish to contribute.&nbsp;As to your future employment, as we have previously explained, you are obliged to work until end of your notice period, in accordance with the terms of your contract.&nbsp; Once your notice period has expired, you will be free to commence working for Johnson Law Group, together with Peter Gallagher, should you so choose.&nbsp; In this specific and limited regard, your covenants will not be enforced.Finally, the firm is very mindful of its professional and regulatory responsibilities and would never expect you to do anything to compromise those. The firm will always supervise its lawyers and safeguard them from potential breaches, and we trust you will do so at all times too, both on your own behalf and on behalf of those you supervise. We do not intend doing anything, nor have we done anything, that would put us or you in breach of any regulatory or professional requirements which remain an absolute priority to us. Everything we have done or are intending to do is with the best interests of our clients as well as our staff in mind first and foremost.We very much hope that this email can now draw these discussions to a conclusion so that we all focus on the imminent trial and delivering the best results to our clients.Best, Anna&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Anna&nbsp;VargaPartner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7890&nbsp;043&nbsp;699From:&nbsp;Michael Burke &lt;mburke@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Sent:&nbsp;Friday, October 3, 2025 12:28 PMTo:&nbsp;Alicia Alinia &lt;aalinia@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Howard Morris &lt;hmorris@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Joseph Moreno &lt;jmoreno@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Anna Varga &lt;avarga@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Cc:&nbsp;Priyanka Gladson &lt;pgladson@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Matt Hunt &lt;mhunt@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Georgina Fallows &lt;gfallows@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;; Sarah Bingham &lt;sbingham@pogustgoodhead.com&gt;Subject:&nbsp;LD Agency&nbsp;Dear Board, Anna,&nbsp;We have been sent a copy of the draft Agency Agreement by LD late last night and we are considering this. As an initial point, the definition of &quot;Common Work&quot; is not wide enough to capture all common work. It is currently a vague halfway house that doesn't offer clarity or certainty, and we suspect will further encourage the Defendants (and other interested parties) to complain and/or ask more questions.&nbsp;In addition, we cannot stress enough how traumatic we and the entire team are finding this process, and that it is happening on the eve of trial. That is exacerbated by the fact that we are only properly notified of proposed fundamental changes to our employment through Martyn Day and his team, who as a third party, is not our employer, and where it appears to us these changes are being pushed through as a fait accompli publicly, yet without the case leads consultation and agreement.&nbsp;It is massively unfair on each of us, it is not creating a good working environment and may prejudice our ability to produce the level of work required for this trial.&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Before we are in a position to give our agreement (which for the avoidance of doubt we consider necessary, as with all named personnel, before the agreement is capable of execution), we have a number of points that require clarification:&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; we be provided sight of the advice from Ben Williams KC (and any other applicable counsel) who have advised on the AA;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; before the agreement is consented to (at least by the case leads) that there is an agreed and defined role for Pete Gallagher in the individual and pan-NOx litigations, documented (and signed) alongside the AA;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; how the claimant committees, and wider claimant cohort, will be informed that the teams with conduct of their cases are under dual supervision of Martyn Day, and Alicia Alina, in respect of the different spheres of work (common / generic); and·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A &quot;step back&quot; clause is included such as to allow the case leads or team to step back from the AA if the personnel consider their regulatory / professional obligations are untenable with instructions given to them.&nbsp;We also note that the intended agreement covers a list of PG personnel wider than just the case leads (the list provided by LD to Michael Burke at 8.23PM last night has 18 people on it). If it is thought that the list should be wider than just the case leads, which we have expressly disagreed with, we are concerned that by not including the entire department, it creates a two-tier team in PG which will not be conducive for anyone. To seek to split the team will only exacerbate this as it risks alienating individuals. We have genuine concerns for the mental health and well-being of our staff in any event with the ongoing upheaval and the constant attacks by the press and Defendants, and to create an artificial split among the team risks compounding the issues that they may be having. The list of PG personnel should either be only the case leads of Michael Burke, Priyanka Gladson, Georgie Fallows, Matthew Hunt and Sarah Bingham (our preference), or the entire team.&nbsp;Additional to this, we trust that each individual will have their individual consent sought before the agreement can be put in place. If the agreement is sought to be incepted prior to consent being sought, this could constitute a breach of contract for all those it is purported to apply to.&nbsp;Thanks,&nbsp;Case Leads&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;​​​​Michael&nbsp;Burke(He/Him)Partner+44&nbsp;(0)&nbsp;7960&nbsp;032&nbsp;34407960&nbsp;032&nbsp;344&nbsp;_____________________________________________________ If our account details change, we will notify these to you by letter, telephone or face-to-face and never by email. Clyde &amp; Co’s privacy notice can be found here Privacy notice: Clyde &amp; Co (clydeco.com) Please could we request that all correspondence with us is sent electronically where possible. For matters subject to the jurisdiction of England &amp; Wales and where you have agreed with the lawyer with the conduct of the case that email service is appropriate, please use this address service@clydeco.com for service, subject to the restrictions set out here . Please copy your email to the lawyer with conduct of the case and include our Clyde &amp; Co reference. This email is sent for and on behalf of Clyde &amp; Co LLP, a limited liability partnership registered in England and Wales under number OC326539 and with its registered office at The St Botolph Building, 138 Houndsditch, London, EC3A 7AR, United Kingdom (Tel: +44 20 7876 5000. Fax: +44 20 7876 5111). Clyde &amp; Co LLP is authorised and regulated by the Solicitors Regulation Authority under number 460690 and uses the word &quot;partner&quot; to refer to a member of the LLP, or an employee or consultant with equivalent standing and qualifications. A list of members is available at: www.clydeco.com. This email and any attachments are confidential and may also be privileged. If you have received this message in error, please notify the sender immediately, destroy this email and any attachments, and do not use, copy, store and/or disclose to any person this email and any attachments.