 team, who as a third party, is not our employer, and where it appears to us these changes are being pushed through as a fait accompli publicly, yet without the case leads consultation and agreement.&nbsp;It is massively unfair on each of us, it is not creating a good working environment and may prejudice our ability to produce the level of work required for this trial.&nbsp;&nbsp; &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;Before we are in a position to give our agreement (which for the avoidance of doubt we consider necessary, as with all named personnel, before the agreement is capable of execution), we have a number of points that require clarification:&nbsp;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; we be provided sight of the advice from Ben Williams KC (and any other applicable counsel) who have advised on the AA;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; before the agreement is consented to (at least by the case leads) that there is an agreed and defined role for Pete Gallagher in the individual and pan-NOx litigations, documented (and signed) alongside the AA;·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; how the claimant committees, and wider claimant cohort, will be informed that the teams with conduct of their cases are under dual supervision of Martyn Day, and Alicia Alina, in respect of the different spheres of work (common / generic); and·&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp; A &quot;step back&quot; clause is included such as to allow the case leads or team to step back from the AA if the personnel consider their regulatory / professional obligations are untenable with instructions given to them.&nbsp;We also note that the intended agreement covers a list of PG personnel wider than just the case leads (the list provided by LD to Michael Burke at 8.23PM last night has 18 people on it). If it is thought that the list should be wider than just the case leads, which we have expressly disagreed with, we are concerned that by not including the entire department, it creates a two-tier team in PG which 