&nbsp;
Hi all,
&nbsp;
Proposed agenda below, which is largely a recirculation of my email from earlier in the week.&nbsp; The goal is for us to expend minimal resources
 to keep up pressure on the Defendants (e.g. sending them weekly lists and passing on journalist requests for documents), whilst allowing us to get on with trial.&nbsp; I think that we all agree that we shouldn't park this all until next summer, and that arguing
 everything now is a massive distraction from the actual trial.
&nbsp;


Decide our position in respect of each of the items below: keep arguing, park for now, take more time to think about it.&nbsp; We may decide that we need more time to consider and/or
 the silks' views on some items.
Decide what to say about each item in our draft letter, which PG and LD have seen and commented on: ​​2025.10.28
 Letter from PG (on behalf of Lead Claimants) to Lead Defendants.docx​​
&nbsp;
I am keen on sending today a single letter, as comprehensive as possible, to the Defendants and third parties, which we can point to in order
 to answer the constant questions about what is happening with the third party folder.&nbsp; I am slightly selfishly interested in that because it would mean that I can get on with PCD (Gareth is probably in an even worse position), but it would also be a useful
 tool for the Court and third parties.
&nbsp;


Non-controversial Green documents. &nbsp;KEEP



These are in a new draft folder that we have set up (the &quot;NOx Disclosure Folder&quot;) and shared with the Defendants for comments, which we have requested be sent by 4pm
 today in order that we may update the Court next week.




&nbsp;



What to do with controversial Green documents. &nbsp;PARK



I think that we have agreed to park arguing about these.



&nbsp;
Redactions. &nbsp;TBD



We have conceded that redactions can be made but have not worked out a full reply to VW's proposal.&nbsp; When we send the weekly lists of documents to the Defendants, can we also
 send them the documents, and they then have three days, say, to send us redacted versions for upload to the NOx Disclosure Folder?
VW proposed redacting two documents almost two weeks ago and haven't provided them.&nbsp; They should at 4pm today in response to the draft NOx Disclosure Folder.
Offer to send VW a link to the Opus folder containing the cartel material so that they can review it ahead of time.


&nbsp;



&nbsp;
Sending weekly lists of Green and Amber documents to the Defendants. &nbsp;KEEP



I think we agree that we should keep doing this.&nbsp; We are producing the lists anyway to track collateral use, it maintains pressure on the Defendants, and it is the only way
 to generate the lists of agreed content for the NOx Disclosure Folder, and then Defendants' responses in which they set out very narrow interpretations of what has been referred to.



&nbsp;
Transcripts. &nbsp;KEEP



My view is that these should go into the NOx Disclosure Folder and we should fight to put them there.&nbsp; The Defendants are ignoring us on this point, third parties have requested
 them, it puts a lot of pressure on the Defendants, and does not require much resource to keep arguing about.



&nbsp;
Amber documents: the Defendants' lists of which parts have been referred to - what to do with those parts once the application deadline passes without an application. &nbsp;KEEP



Since these are agreed they can go into the NOx Disclosure Folder.&nbsp; They will be heavily redacted documents.&nbsp; We proposed this to the Defendants when we shared with them the
 draft NOx Disclosure Folder.



&nbsp;
Amber documents: how to respond to all other parts not in the Defendants' lists. &nbsp;PARK



This is a significant piece of work. &nbsp;I think that we have the resources for each solicitor OEM team (noting Gareth's point about the juniors), once their portion of the trial
 is done, to come up with a counter list of which parts of amber documents were referred to.&nbsp; The hardest job will be for Nissan, as they are last and have the largest number of Amber documents, but the PCD team only has three documents to consider and Merc
 can start in a fortnight.



&nbsp;
Documents requested by third parties, whether Green or Amber. &nbsp;TBD



We have proposed putting these in a special category, i.e., not parking them: when a third party requests a document, the Defendants have seven days to make an application.&nbsp;
 I think that is reasonable and worth raising at least once.&nbsp; The question is what to do when the Defendants reply refusing to treat them differently, or when the Defendants have a very narrow interpretation of what &quot;part&quot; of the requested document was referred
 to.



&nbsp;
Writing to the Court. &nbsp;TBD



We need to get out into the open that third parties and journalists are writing to the Court to request documents.&nbsp; So far I am aware of quite a few requests to the Court for
 documents, but the Defendants are only aware of one, and I do not think that that Cockerill J is aware of any of them.&nbsp; Francesca Girardot (one of Cockerill J's assistant lawyers) has said that she is not troubling the Judge with requests at the moment.&nbsp; I
 propose a neutral email to the Court saying, the NOx Disclosure Folder exists, who do you want us to share it with?



&nbsp;
How and when to resolve things that are parked. &nbsp;TBD



Written submissions would require less from counsel - we have a lot from the DDH order submissions that we can repackage.&nbsp; It would also require less from third parties, who
 ideally would do most of the running on this.
Constable J.
Ideally early next year.



&nbsp;
Raising the issue in Court. &nbsp;TBD



I agree with Ben that Cockerill J is liable to make unhelpful comments, and even if we found time to take her through the DDH judgment and order the Defendants would just disagree.&nbsp;
 But, once we have agreed our proposal for all of the above, I think that it is sensible to update her, in particular to tell her that the NOx Disclosure Folder has been set up, and ask her whether the Court could pass on any requests that third parties make
 for documents to the Claimants and Defendants.

